__d(function(e,t,a,o,n,i,r){n.exports={gardens:[{id:"1",name:"Duncan Garden",desc:"Stand at the rim of Duncan Garden and you are looking into a hole that was dug by hand. It was called the Sunken Garden, and it sank because Manito's first superintendent, Charles Balzer, hauled 42,500 wagon loads of soil out of it \u2014 earth that went on to build the gardens of parks all over the city. In 1912 the park's second superintendent, John Duncan, laid out the three acres in front of you in the formal European manner: bilateral symmetry, a central water feature, geometrical planting beds. He served from 1910 to 1942, and in 1941, while he was still in the job, the garden was renamed in his honor. The fountain at the center was a gift from Mrs. L. M. Davenport in memory of her husband Louis. Given in 1955, it is 'Mt Airy' light-grey granite topped with bronze swans, designed by the Spokane firm Whitehouse & Price. The gazebo at the south end was built to celebrate Manito's centennial. Around them the turf is kept close-cropped and the beds are turned over to colorful annuals each season. When you're ready, follow the axis north to the glass house standing at the head of the garden.",coord:[47.6344353,-117.4117362],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15",extraSource:"https://www.dahp.wa.gov/sites/default/files/FINAL_OlmstedSpokane_Manito.pdf"},{id:"2",name:"Gaiser Conservatory",desc:"Step through the door of the Gaiser Conservatory and the air changes \u2014 warm, wet and green, in a city that spends half the year under snow. A conservatory has stood on this spot since 1912. The one around you is younger: an all-aluminum house and its working greenhouses, designed and built in 1974 and funded entirely by private donations. In 1988 the central dome was redesigned and enlarged, then named in memory of Dr. David Gaiser, a civic leader and a patron of this park. Under that dome a waterfall spills into a pond and large tropical plants reach for the glass. The flowering displays are changed throughout the year, so no two visits look quite alike \u2014 and in December the whole house turns red with poinsettias. Back outside, a little further north, the planting loosens up completely. That is the Ferris Perennial Garden.",coord:[47.6354844,-117.4117363],practical:"Open 9:00 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. daily, closed Wednesdays for routine maintenance. Closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\u2019s Day.",source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"3",name:"Joel E. Ferris Perennial Garden",desc:"If Duncan Garden is the park in its dress uniform, the Ferris Garden is the park off duty. John Duncan established it in 1940 as an informal counterweight to the formality below \u2014 no axis, no symmetry, just drifts of perennials doing what perennials do. It was later named in honor of Joel E. Ferris, a former park board member and a popular civic leader. Over 300 plant species grow here, which is why the garden is never the same twice: something is always coming into flower as something else goes over, straight through the season. In recent years its structure has evolved again, with larger beds, small trees worked in among them, and a multitude of exciting grasses \u2014 so stand still a moment and listen to it as well as look at it. From here the ground rises to the west, toward the roses.",coord:[47.636333,-117.4114895],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"4",name:"Rose Hill",desc:"Rose Hill is a garden that one man imagined and never got to finish. John Duncan first conceived it before his retirement. The work itself came in the late 1940s, developed as a joint venture between the Spokane Rose Society and City of Spokane Parks and Recreation \u2014 volunteers and city crews planting side by side. The gardens now hold 150 varieties of hybrid tea, grandiflora, floribunda and miniature roses. At the north end a pergola honors the late photographer Erna Bert Nelson, a generous benefactor to Spokane Parks, and the sundial nearby is dedicated to the memory of Jacob and Ward Wortman, the sons of Mr. and Mrs. R. Jackson Wortman. A second pergola went up in the park's centennial year with funding from The Friends of Manito and the Spokane Rose Society, and a new gazebo followed in 2015, funded by The Friends. The hill has never really stopped being built. Walk west now, and the mood changes entirely.",coord:[47.6362408,-117.4127233],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15",sourceNote:"The City brochure says Rose Hill was \"developed in the late 1940s\". The National Register nomination is more precise: conceived by Duncan before his retirement, carried out after the war, and opened officially on 8 September 1952, with about 1,500 bushes in by 1954.",extraSource:"https://www.dahp.wa.gov/sites/default/files/FINAL_OlmstedSpokane_Manito.pdf"},{id:"5",name:"Nishinomiya-Tsutakawa Japanese Gardens",desc:"Twelve years of planning went into this garden before it became a reality in the spring of 1974. Its elements have been artistically placed in an asymmetric, strolling pond style, so the path bends, a view is withheld and then given, and every stone and pine is placed rather than simply planted. It is meant as an intimate reminder of nature, inspiring tranquility and peace, and most people find they slow down here without deciding to. The garden is also a piece of diplomacy: it symbolizes the friendship of Spokane and its sister city, Nishinomiya. In December 2007 it was named to honor Ed Tsutakawa, who founded the Spokane-Nishinomiya sister city relationship and championed the sister city movement in Spokane for 45 years, until his death that October. In 2008 The Journal of Japanese Gardening ranked it 16th among more than 300 public Japanese gardens. The design has since been updated with a new, more accessible entrance. Take your time. When you leave, the lilacs are northeast of here.",coord:[47.6362517,-117.4161136],practical:"Open daily April through October, 10:00 a.m. to 6:00 p.m. Personal photography is welcome; commercial and portrait photography, tripods and reflectors are not permitted in the garden.",source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15",sourceNote:"The project began in 1962, the year after the sister-city agreement. The city retained designer Nagao Sakurai -- who had designed the Emperor\u2019s Garden in Tokyo -- in 1967; he suffered a stroke while working in Spokane and died before the garden opened.",extraSource:"https://www.dahp.wa.gov/sites/default/files/FINAL_OlmstedSpokane_Manito.pdf"},{id:"6",name:"Lilac Garden",desc:"Spokane calls itself the Lilac City, and this hillside is a large part of the reason. In 1912 John Duncan sent away to Rochester, New York, and obtained 128 named lilac cultivars \u2014 that shipment was the beginning of the Lilac Garden. Today it contains well over 100 named cultivars from 23 distinct species, making it one of the most important lilac gardens in the west. In 2003 the Spokane Lilac Society made a contribution to Manito Park for an extension of the garden, and growing in it is Syringa vulgaris 'Spokane', a lovely double pink lilac that carries the city's name. Come back in May, when the whole slope is scented, and the nickname will need no explaining at all. From here the park opens out to the east, toward the picnic grounds at 18th and Grand.",coord:[47.6373343,-117.4133321],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"7",name:"Picnic Shelter",desc:"Every park needs a room without walls, and this is Manito's. The Rotary Club Picnic Shelter stands in the large picnic grounds area near 18th and Grand. It was built in 1961 as a gift from the Rotary Clubs of Spokane to the City, in commemoration of the club's 50th anniversary \u2014 a birthday present handed to a whole city. Underneath it are charcoal grills and picnic tables for public use, and there are playground facilities near the shelter, which is why on a summer Saturday you will hear this stop before you see it. When the smoke and the shouting are behind you, walk west toward the water.",coord:[47.6382633,-117.4082762],practical:"To rent the picnic shelter, or to reserve the gardens for a wedding, call 509.363.5455.",source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"8",name:"Mirror Pond",desc:"This was once a lake \u2014 a natural one, called Mirror Lake \u2014 and every summer it quietly dried up. While the water lasted it was large enough to accommodate canoes, which could be rented on site. A dance hall stood on the bank, and from 1905 to 1907 open-air motion pictures were shown nearby: a Spokane crowd out in the dark at the edge of the water, watching pictures move. Later a concrete retaining structure was built to keep the pond full throughout the year, and the seasonal lake became the permanent one in front of you. The canoes and the dance hall are long gone. The ducks stayed, and Mirror Pond is now a favorite meeting place for anyone who enjoys watching them and the other water fowl \u2014 with one request that comes with the view: please don\u2019t feed the water fowl. Follow the shore south, and you will come to a small building with a long history of its own.",coord:[47.6386356,-117.4109182],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"9",name:"Park Bench Cafe",desc:"This little building was built in 1923, and its first name was the Peanut Shack. Its original purpose was to provide refreshments for zoo visitors and peanuts for the monkeys \u2014 because for nearly thirty years, Manito had a zoo. The Manito Park Zoo drew crowds from 1905, and its residents included buffalo, elk, bobcats, cougars, bears and deer as well as those monkeys. The Great Depression ended it: the park board voted to close the zoo in 1932, and the last animals were gone by January 1933. The shack outlived every one of them. It is the Park Bench Caf\xe9 now, and it remains a favorite summer spot for locals and tourists alike \u2014 so when you sit outside with something cold, you are sitting where people once queued for a bag of peanuts. Just north of here, the old road begins.",coord:[47.6371554,-117.4102718],practical:"The caf\xe9 is open June through September, and may close occasionally due to weather.",source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",extraSource:"https://spokanehistorical.org/items/show/105",sourceNote:"The City brochure dates the zoo's demise to 1933; Spokane Historical and the park board's own vote date it to 1932. Both are right -- the vote was 1932, the animals were destroyed in January 1933.",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"10",name:"Loop Drive and Bridge",desc:"Loop Drive was built for cars and has been handed back to people. Now closed to vehicular traffic, it provides a scenic route for pedestrians to get from the Park Bench Caf\xe9 and Ferris Perennial Garden up to Rose Hill, with the Lilac Garden and Mirror Pond opening below you as you climb. The picturesque stone bridge is the thing to stop for. It was constructed in the early 1930s, and it showcases the same architectural style found in many of the park's buildings from its early years \u2014 dark basalt, laid heavy and low, built to look as though it had always been there. Stand on it for a moment and look down the draw. Then, if you have the legs for it, the longest walk of the tour: south, to Upper Manito.",coord:[47.6378891,-117.4106714],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"11",name:"Upper Manito Playground",desc:"Charles Balzer \u2014 the same superintendent who emptied Duncan Garden by the wagon load \u2014 originally established Upper Manito in the early 1900s, and he did it with nothing to spend. At that time the City did not provide money for equipment, so Balzer procured power poles and made swings out of them. That improvisation is the ancestor of everything here now: the playground features a softball diamond, playground equipment, a picnic shelter and a splash pad, which make it a favorite gathering spot for children in the warm months. Children have been turning up on this ground for more than a century. One stop remains \u2014 back toward the middle of the park, near the conservatory.",coord:[47.6326965,-117.4098775],source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"},{id:"12",name:"Administrative Offices",desc:"The tour ends at a working building. This one was built of natural basalt rock in 1912 \u2014 the local stone, the same stone as the bridge on Loop Drive. That year also gave Manito its first conservatory, its first 128 lilacs, and the formal garden John Duncan laid out below. Inside, it provides office space for the Park Department horticulture staff and The Friends of Manito. They are the people who plan the beds, keep the glass house warm through a Spokane winter, and raise the money for the pergolas and gazebos you have been walking past all along. None of what you have seen today maintains itself. When you leave, the paths run back down toward Duncan Garden, and whatever is in bloom will be different the next time you come.",coord:[47.6358505,-117.411739],practical:"For reservations and information, call 509.363.5455.",source:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",verified:"2026-08-15"}],amenities:[{id:"garden_2",title:"Duncan Garden",type:"garden",coord:[47.6344353,-117.4117362]},{id:"garden_3",title:"Gaiser Conservatory",type:"garden",coord:[47.6354844,-117.4117363]},{id:"garden_4",title:"Joel E. Ferris Perennial Garden",type:"garden",coord:[47.636333,-117.4114895]},{id:"garden_5",title:"Rose Hill",type:"garden",coord:[47.6362408,-117.4127233]},{id:"garden_6",title:"Nishinomiya-Tsutakawa Japanese Gardens",type:"garden",coord:[47.6362517,-117.4161136]},{id:"garden_7",title:"Lilac Garden",type:"garden",coord:[47.6373343,-117.4133321]},{id:"garden_8",title:"Picnic Shelter",type:"garden",coord:[47.6382633,-117.4082762]},{id:"garden_9",title:"Mirror Pond",type:"garden",coord:[47.6386356,-117.4109182]},{id:"garden_10",title:"Park Bench Cafe",type:"garden",coord:[47.6371554,-117.4102718]},{id:"garden_11",title:"Loop Drive and Bridge",type:"garden",coord:[47.6378891,-117.4106714]},{id:"garden_12",title:"Upper Manito Playground",type:"garden",coord:[47.6326965,-117.4098775]},{id:"garden_13",title:"Administrative Offices",type:"garden",coord:[47.6358505,-117.411739]},{id:"13",title:"Restroom",type:"bathroom",coord:[47.6329712,-117.4103683]},{id:"14",title:"Restroom",type:"bathroom",coord:[47.6358992,-117.4114895]},{id:"15",title:"Restroom",type:"bathroom",coord:[47.6383282,-117.4102664]},{id:"16",title:"Restroom",type:"bathroom",coord:[47.6362282,-117.415494]},{id:"17",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6361233,-117.4148744]},{id:"18",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6358269,-117.4128467]},{id:"19",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6366258,-117.4136782]},{id:"20",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6362499,-117.4119455]},{id:"21",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6389211,-117.4101967]},{id:"22",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.638903,-117.4080294]},{id:"23",title:"Parking lot",type:"parking",coord:[47.6331375,-117.4099231]},{id:"24",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6388994,-117.4100626]},{id:"25",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6389536,-117.4077129]},{id:"26",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6364668,-117.4136728]},{id:"27",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6364487,-117.4163711]},{id:"28",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6344605,-117.4122834]},{id:"29",title:"Handicap Accessable",type:"accessibility",coord:[47.6329856,-117.4094564]},{id:"30",title:"Drinking Fountain",type:"water",coord:[47.6358432,-117.4114895]},{id:"31",title:"Drinking Fountain",type:"water",coord:[47.6371066,-117.4124658]},{id:"32",title:"Drinking Fountain",type:"water",coord:[47.6382199,-117.4094832]},{id:"33",title:"Drinking Fountain",type:"water",coord:[47.6328122,-117.4093437]},{id:"34",title:"Informational Kiosk",type:"info",coord:[47.6364198,-117.4159098]},{id:"35",title:"Informational Kiosk",type:"info",coord:[47.6361631,-117.4118972]},{id:"36",title:"Informational Kiosk",type:"info",coord:[47.6373921,-117.4102396]},{id:"37",title:"Informational Kiosk",type:"info",coord:[47.6388271,-117.4084961]},{id:"38",title:"Informational Kiosk",type:"info",coord:[47.6329242,-117.4101484]}],meta:{narrativeSource:"https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/parks/manito/manito-park-walking-tour-brochure-2025-08-22.pdf",narrativeSourceName:"City of Spokane, \"A Walking Tour of Manito Park\", August 2025 edition",verified:"2026-08-15",narrativeRewritten:"2026-08-23",note:"Garden narratives are adapted from the City\u2019s current walking-tour brochure: every fact is the City\u2019s, the ordering and the wording are ours. They were rewritten on 2026-08-23 as spoken arcs -- hook, history, what is in front of you, handoff to the next stop -- because the brochure text read as a stack of unordered facts when narrated aloud. Nothing dated or numeric was added in the rewrite; `verified` is still the date the facts were last checked against the brochure. Re-check `practical` fields against the brochure each season -- those are what go stale first."}}},713,[]);
